<CaseStudy
  title="Validation-First Trading Research Platforms"
  description="Two private Python platforms (equities and crypto) built to test trading strategies honestly and to refuse to go live when they fail. Both rejected every strategy tested."
>
  <p class="lede">
    Two private Python platforms built to test strategies honestly, and to refuse to go live when they fail.
  </p>
  <p>
    These are <strong>not profitable trading bots</strong>. They are research platforms built to answer one question properly:
    {' '}<em>does a strategy have a real edge after costs, risk and overfitting, and if not, when should it be kept away from real money?</em>{' '}
    Both platforms answered "no" for every strategy tested, and both are built to act on that answer.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Equities system:</strong> daily swing trading across the full US and UK stock market (about 6,700 common stocks), paper-trading first.</li>
    <li><strong>Crypto system:</strong> Bitcoin spot research with a buy-and-hold monthly DCA fallback.</li>
    <li><strong>Repositories:</strong> private. Code walkthrough available on request.</li>
    <li><strong>Period:</strong> built Oct to Nov 2026 (182 and 83 commits).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>At a glance (verified 23 Nov 2026)</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption class="sr-only">Comparison of the two systems</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Equities system</th><th scope="col">Crypto system</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Size</th><td>about 46,000 lines of Python in 124 modules</td><td>about 41,000 lines in 140 modules</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Automated tests</th><td>1,683</td><td>1,092</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Test run</th><td><strong>1,682 passed, 1 failed</strong> (a stale hard-coded trade count in one fixture test, not a code defect; to be updated)</td><td><strong>1,088 passed, 4 skipped</strong> (tests that need real keys or a network, off by default)</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Outcome</th><td>Backtest lost money after costs and <strong>failed 5 of 6 go-live gates</strong></td><td><strong>Every strategy rejected</strong>, no champion, fallback stays buy-and-hold DCA, sealed holdout never used</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How it was built</h2>
  <p>
    Built with <strong>AI coding agents working from written specifications</strong>, a rules file, and automated tests. I wrote the specifications,
    the governance rules and the decision log, set the acceptance criteria, and reviewed the results. The agents wrote most of the code. Because of that,
    the design leans heavily on tests, pre-registered protocols and rules that agents are forbidden to change.
  </p>

  <h2>What the platforms do</h2>
  <p>Both systems share the same philosophy. Where a feature belongs to one system only, it is marked.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Data layer.</strong>
      <ul>
        <li><em>Equities:</em> a full-market universe built from the official Nasdaq Trader and London Stock Exchange instrument lists, with a cached daily price store and a nightly screening funnel.</li>
        <li><em>Crypto:</em> point-in-time-safe data where every feature declares when it became available, archive checksums, timestamp normalisation and lagged alternative data, so nothing from the future leaks into a signal.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Research and validation.</strong>
      <ul>
        <li><em>Both:</em> a <strong>sealed holdout ("lockbox")</strong> that research may not touch, a research ledger that counts every trial, rule ablations and Monte Carlo resampling for drawdown and risk of ruin.</li>
        <li><em>Equities:</em> binding go-live gates (profit factor, expectancy, drawdown, years tested, trade count, and Sharpe against the S&amp;P 500 over the same dates).</li>
        <li><em>Crypto:</em> rolling walk-forward testing with purged and embargoed splits, a lockbox that can be opened at most three times, and overfitting statistics: probability of backtest overfitting (PBO via CSCV), deflated Sharpe ratio and Hansen's SPA test.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Decision and sizing.</strong> Risk-based position sizing, circuit breakers on drawdown and losing streaks, and an LLM judgement step that may only veto or halve a trade, never add one.</li>
    <li><strong>Execution safety.</strong>
      <ul>
        <li><em>Both:</em> dry-run by default (equities against the Trading 212 demo environment, crypto against the exchange testnet), a kill switch, and protective stops required after every fill.</li>
        <li><em>Crypto:</em> exchange-side protective stops, deterministic client order IDs with query-before-retry after timeouts, and several independent conditions that must all hold before any live order.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Governance.</strong> Hypotheses are written down before they are tested. Agents may not edit gate thresholds, safety limits or gate code to make a strategy pass. A failed gate is an expected, reportable result.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Results, stated plainly</h2>
  <h3>Equities backtest</h3>
  <p>2 Jan 2024 to 13 Nov 2026, 2.86 years, 244 closed trades, costs included.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption class="sr-only">Equities backtest results</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Net result on money deposited</td><td><strong>-2.9%</strong> (time-weighted return -11.5%)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Expectancy</td><td><strong>-0.12R</strong> per trade</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Profit factor</td><td><strong>0.89</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maximum drawdown</td><td>17.4%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>S&amp;P 500 buy-and-hold, same dates</td><td><strong>+59%</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trading costs vs gross gains</td><td>Costs were larger than the gross gains</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Go-live gates</td><td><strong>Failed 5 of 6</strong> (only "enough trades" passed)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A diagnostic flagged that the setup score was <strong>anti-predictive</strong> on this window: higher-scored setups did worse than lower-scored ones.
    The system reported this itself instead of hiding it.
  </p>

  <h3>Crypto research</h3>
  <p>Walk-forward, pre-registered protocols, sealed holdout untouched.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The trend strategy (S1) cut maximum drawdown by more than half compared with buy-and-hold, but earned less after UK tax and <strong>failed the overfitting checks</strong> (PBO about 0.53, SPA p-value 1.0).</li>
    <li>The swing strategies (S3 to S5) failed on costs (unprofitable at double costs), sample size and the overfitting tests.</li>
    <li>Conclusion recorded in the decision log: <strong>no champion; the fallback stays buy-and-hold DCA.</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    During the crypto research I also found and fixed a <strong>double-counted fee</strong> in the evaluation engine.
    The fix removed an error and changed no threshold, and it was logged as such.
  </p>

  <h2>What this demonstrates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Designing an evaluation that can say "no": pre-registration, trial accounting, sealed holdouts, honest baselines (buy-and-hold with identical cash flows) and, in the crypto system, formal multiple-testing control.</li>
    <li>Treating negative results as results. Neither platform was tuned until it passed.</li>
    <li>Production-minded engineering around ML and quantitative code: leakage prevention, fail-closed execution, idempotent order handling, extensive tests.</li>
    <li>Working with AI coding agents safely: written specs, rules agents cannot override, tests as the arbiter, and human review of decisions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Backtests and research only. Both systems are configured for dry-run, and no live trading has been enabled because the go-live gates fail.</li>
    <li>A single developer reviewing largely AI-written code: the large test suites are the main safeguard, not a substitute for independent review.</li>
    <li>The equities backtest window (2.86 years) is shorter than the five-year gate the project set itself.</li>
    <li>The strategies and parameters are deliberately not published.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Skills:</strong> Python, pytest, time-series validation, backtesting, Monte Carlo simulation, statistical testing for overfitting (PBO, deflated Sharpe, SPA), risk and position sizing, defensive system design, AI-assisted development.</p>
</CaseStudy>
